    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build passes and the full test suite is green \u2014 `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and both its tests pass (`coreDerivesRegionForKnownCity` \u2192 NSW, `functionalityUnknownCityIsUnknown` \u2192 UNKNOWN).\n\n## Summary\n\n`locality` is a pure function of an owner's already-stored `city`, surfaced only in the response \u2014 architecturally identical to the existing derived fields (`displayName`, `initials`, `membershipTier`), which are computed at mapping time rather than persisted. I followed that pattern rather than adding a stored entity column or a create-pipeline step (which would be redundant denormalization, since nothing about locality depends on other owners or creation-time state).\n\nThree focused changes:\n\n1. **`util/CityRegion.java`** (new) \u2014 a small single-responsibility helper holding the pinned city\u2192region table (`Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD`) and `CityRegion.of(city)`, returning the canonical region or `\"UNKNOWN\"` (also for a null/absent city). Kept separate so the mapper doesn't accumulate a lookup table, and reusable if future region-derived rules (timezone, postcode) land.\n\n2. **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `@Mapping(target = \"locality\", expression = \"java(locality(owner))\")` and a one-line `locality(owner)` default method delegating to `CityRegion.of(...)`, mirroring the surrounding derived-field methods.\n\n3. **`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `locality` string property to the `Owner` schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field), alongside the other derived read-only fields.\n\nNo changes to the create pipeline, entity, or persistence were needed \u2014 locality is derived on every read straight from the city.",
